WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:06.263
Bacteria can inherit genes from other bacteria.

2
00:00:07.523 --> 00:00:08.723
DNA arrives by three routes.

3
00:00:10.043 --> 00:00:12.463
Transformation takes up free DNA from the surroundings.

4
00:00:13.583 --> 00:00:16.163
Conjugation passes DNA through cell contact.

5
00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:21.343
Transduction brings DNA inside a phage, a virus that infects bacteria.

6
00:00:22.323 --> 00:00:25.843
This exchange gives bacteria new genes, even without meiosis.

7
00:00:26.843 --> 00:00:31.623
It also lets geneticists map genes by watching what enters a cell, and which genes

8
00:00:31.623 --> 00:00:32.623
travel together.

9
00:00:33.417 --> 00:00:36.137
In E. coli, F is a small DNA circle.

10
00:00:37.017 --> 00:00:41.137
When F transfers itself, an F− recipient becomes F+.

11
00:00:41.137 --> 00:00:43.597
Chromosomal genes move only rarely.

12
00:00:44.537 --> 00:00:45.857
But F can join the chromosome.

13
00:00:46.757 --> 00:00:48.297
That donor is called Hfr.

14
00:00:49.417 --> 00:00:52.757
Transfer now pulls chromosomal genes across in a fixed order.

15
00:00:53.677 --> 00:00:58.417
Pairs usually break before all of F arrives, so recipients rarely become F+.

16
00:00:59.367 --> 00:01:01.127
Phages move host genes too.

17
00:01:02.027 --> 00:01:06.807
In generalized transduction, a phage sometimes packs a host chromosome fragment

18
00:01:06.807 --> 00:01:08.427
instead of its own DNA.

19
00:01:09.487 --> 00:01:10.487
Any part can be carried.

20
00:01:11.387 --> 00:01:15.687
In specialized transduction, lambda leaves its chromosome attachment site

21
00:01:15.687 --> 00:01:18.027
inaccurately and takes a neighbouring gene.

22
00:01:18.027 --> 00:01:21.427
Only genes beside that site can move this way.

23
00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:25.330
Delivery and stable inheritance are different steps.

24
00:01:26.210 --> 00:01:30.610
A linear donor fragment must recombine into the matching stretch of the recipient

25
00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.970
chromosome to be inherited stably.

26
00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:38.770
On a circular chromosome, that takes an even number of crossovers, at least two, one

27
00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:39.770
on each side.

28
00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:43.033
Selection makes rare recombinants visible.

29
00:01:43.933 --> 00:01:46.353
Only cells with every selected ability grow.

30
00:01:47.253 --> 00:01:49.433
Interrupted mating uses this readout.

31
00:01:50.293 --> 00:01:55.093
Break Hfr pairs apart at set times and score which donor genes the recipients

32
00:01:55.093 --> 00:01:59.113
inherited. A colony reports both entry and integration.

33
00:02:00.217 --> 00:02:05.517
Markers a, b, c and d first appear at 8, 14, 20 and 31 minutes.

34
00:02:06.357 --> 00:02:09.717
With steady transfer, their gaps are 6, 6 and 11 minutes.

35
00:02:10.597 --> 00:02:11.597
The gaps are the map.

36
00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:15.910
Another Hfr strain reads the same map backwards.

37
00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:21.810
Different origins and orientations give overlapping orders that close into a circle.

38
00:02:22.883 --> 00:02:24.863
Phage transfer gives a finer map.

39
00:02:25.803 --> 00:02:28.823
Genes carried together in a single particle must be close.

40
00:02:29.863 --> 00:02:31.983
Closer genes travel together more often.

41
00:02:32.843 --> 00:02:37.443
These frequencies compare distances, but ordering genes needs three-factor counts.
